Thank you so much.

59
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:27,420
It's always great to hear from folks and it's always appreciated, and it's not that hard

60
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:30,900
to get in touch with me and I'll tell you how at the end of the podcast.

61
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:36,700
So on with the series. this week we're talking about the players of the game, our scouts.

62
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:37,900
So let's get started, shall we?

63
00:03:46,900 --> 00:04:04,660
We began this series by talking about scouting as a game.

64
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:12,900
We talked about our role as the role that's kind of like being a coach, and this week

65
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:16,420
we're going to talk about who plays the game,

66
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:18,220
And that's our scouts.

67
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:22,220
Last week, when we were talking about our role in the game, I was talking a lot about perspective,

68
00:04:22,220 --> 00:04:25,940
and perspective is crucially important,

69
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:32,620
And I'll venture to guess that I introduce perspectives that are kind of uncommon.

70
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:34,460
I know that's a little difficult. sometimes

71
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:41,380
It may seem like I'm being negative about alternative perspectives, but I'm basing

72
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:49,140
what I'm saying about all this on my understanding of the scouting program, just from study and

73
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:52,940
from looking at the way that it was originally built.

74
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:57,980
Not because I have like a whole lot of nostalgia, for we need to go back to the old days, but

75
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:02,100
that scouting is built on a very strong framework.

76
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:08,300
It's just that that framework gets obscured in the past century of habits and practices

77
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:15,580
and adult initiatives and organizational initiatives, when it's really a very clear and simple kind of thing.

78
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:22,060
And it's important to mention that because that affects your perspective of what's going on.

79
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:28,860
In keeping with the analogy that we've been using in this series about scouting being a game

80
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:31,940
and we being coaches and the scouts being players,

81
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:37,980
Let's take up the perspective of an observer and go look at games being played,

82
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:43,300
And this will be true whether we're on a soccer field, a football field, a basketball court, you know

83
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:47,420
wherever we end up. let's take a look at what's going on.

84
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:52,260
There's a set of rules that define this game that's being played.

85
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:54,980
There's a goal to playing the game,

86
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:58,300
There are obviously players and there are obviously coaches.

87
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:00,340
There's a field of play,

88
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:01,700
There's sidelines,

89
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:06,620
There's bleachers, and we need to ask ourselves the roles of the different people involved.

90
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:13,700
So we look at the spectators and the bleachers and we say, OK, well, they're just watching and enjoying this,

91
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:20,780
and they're participating to some extent because, you know, they have a role in the way that the game unfolds.

92
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:27,380
Coaches are participating in a way because they have a role in the way that the game is played,

93
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:32,460
But the real participants are the players on the field of play.

94
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:36,460
And this fits in with what we were talking about a little bit last week

95
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:41,380
as far as what scouts are and what scouts aren't, what players are and what they aren't.

96
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:45,540
First of all, the players are not clients of the coaches,

97
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:51,980
They're not clients of the spectators and they're not employees of the coaches or the spectators.

98
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:57,580
And I point that out because a lot of times our perspective as scouts- as we look at scouts as

99
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:04,740
clients or employees or soldiers or students or something like that back to back to-

100
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:11,300
are looking at the game just from the outside end, being an observer.

101
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,860
What's essential to the game?

102
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:17,780
What has to be present for us to be able to call this the game?

103
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:19,940
We don't need spectators,

104
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:21,780
We don't need coaches,

105
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:26,580
We don't need an officially marked off field of play,

106
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:30,060
We don't need fancy uniforms and equipment.

107
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:31,500
We need two things:

108
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:35,660
We need the concepts that shape the game.

109
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:42,020
That is what we've been calling rules in our analogy, and we need the players,

110
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:43,500
And that's it.

111
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:45,580
That's all that's needed to play the game.

112
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:48,140
So the most important volunteers in scouting-

113
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:52,540
They have more power and influence than the highest scouting official,

114
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:57,460
because without their participation this whole game just comes to a complete halt.

115
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:03,420
These most important volunteers are scouts- bring endless energy and resources

116
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:07,100
and real transformational power to scouting.

117
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:11,900
They're the only volunteers in scouting we simply can't afford to lose

118
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:15,620
And when we look at the whole game from that perspective,

119
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:20,940
when we look at scouting from that perspective, we understand everything

120
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:25,940
about the game is designed to facilitate them being able to reach the ultimate

121
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:28,340
goal of the game, which is the development of character.

122
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:32,980
And everything else about the game is not all that important,

123
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:37,260
And the essential things, the foundational things, the basic things,

124
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:39,460
are really the most important,

125
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:42,580
And that's what this series is designed to highlight.

126
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:44,980
Let's talk about our scouts for a moment.

127
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:47,660
Why are they volunteering to play the game?

128
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:50,580
Are they doing it because they're conscious of the goal,

129
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:52,900
that it's the development of character?

130
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:55,820
I think that they are aware that that is happening,

131
00:08:56,180 --> 00:08:59,580
but they would be challenged to put it into words or say that that's

132
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:00,980
what's important to them.

133
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:04,660
So if we were to ask the average scout why they are in scouting,

134
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:06,780
they would probably say because it's fun.

135
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:10,300
And then if we ask them what's fun about it? well, they could give us

136
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:14,500
any number of answers, but most of those answers would kind of boil down

137
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:17,300
to the idea that I like to be with my friends,

138
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:19,180
I like to go camping,

139
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:24,620
I like being part of an organized effort group, a tribe if you will.

140
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:28,980
Young people are in scouts because they like to be with their friends

141
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:30,780
and go camping.

142
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:33,700
I don't think that young people articulate

143
00:09:33,980 --> 00:09:37,260
why they're doing what they're doing particularly well.

144
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:42,540
They just know. they just know that they get satisfaction out of participating in it.

145
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:49,060
Now we want to have a slightly more nuanced and advanced idea

146
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:52,180
of exactly what makes them participate,

147
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:56,220
But it doesn't get a whole lot more complicated than that.

148
00:09:56,580 --> 00:10:01,420
I am a scout because I like to hang out with my pals and go camping.

149
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:03,180
There you go.

150
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:06,580
Their motivation is honest and it's simple,

151
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:08,740
And that's why they bring so much energy,

152
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:12,820
And that energy is the transformational power of the program.

153
00:10:13,580 --> 00:10:17,740
Our scouts volunteer to be scouts because they like to hang out

154
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:20,020
with their pals and go camping.

155
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:23,660
So how do we take that tremendous energy

156
00:10:24,460 --> 00:10:28,900
and make it possible for them to realize what they want to do.

157
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:30,939
Well, it's kind of simple, right?

158
00:10:30,939 --> 00:10:34,699
We create a safe environment for them to hang out with their friends and go camping.

159
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:39,060
OK, so you got that right.

160
00:10:39,980 --> 00:10:42,220
We've talked about scouting as being a game.

161
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:44,860
We talked about our role as coaches.

162
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:47,180
We've talked a little bit about the players.

163
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:48,220
Hey, we're done.

164
00:10:48,220 --> 00:10:49,340
There you go,

165
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:50,380
Have fun.

166
00:10:53,180 --> 00:10:56,820
But you're telling me: oh my gosh, it is so much more complicated than that.

167
00:10:57,100 --> 00:10:59,060
Yes, I understand that.

168
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:03,380
OK, but most of the complications are unnecessary.

169
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:05,700
They're just things that we don't need.

170
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:10,180
There are things that don't advance us towards the goal.

171
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:13,540
So if you're a newer listener to the podcast,

172
00:11:13,540 --> 00:11:15,460
you're not going to be familiar with what I'm about to do.

173
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:19,700
but I'm going to pull out the old soapbox and stand up on it

174
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:22,900
And I'm going to rant for a short period of time.

175
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:26,860
I started blogging at ScoutmasterCTcom in 2005..

176
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:31,820
And this is the three hundred and sixty third episode of the Scoutmaster podcast.

177
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:35,380
So I've talked with literally hundreds of Scouters,

178
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:38,060
And before that, for thirty, thirty five years,

179
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:40,940
I worked with thousands of Scouts and Scouters.

180
00:11:41,220 --> 00:11:42,820
And I'm not looking for a pat on the back,

181
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:45,220
I'm just pointing out I've been around for a little bit.

182
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:46,340
I've seen a lot of things.

183
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:47,380
I've heard from a lot of people.

184
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:49,180
I've had a lot of discussions with people.

185
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:54,660
The largest number of problems that people ask for assistance with

186
00:11:54,900 --> 00:11:59,100
are caused by things that do not matter.

187
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:00,860
There I said it.

188
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:06,380
You know, there's one thing that matters in this game

189
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:08,460
and that is reaching the goal.

190
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:12,540
And the only way we reach the goal is if we have Scouts to work with.

191
00:12:12,980 --> 00:12:15,579
So the Scouts are the most important part of this

192
00:12:15,579 --> 00:12:20,060
and their advancement towards the goal is our most important initiative.

193
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:22,380
Everything else is window dressing,

194
00:12:22,380 --> 00:12:24,660
And I mean everything else.

195
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:29,339
The difficulty is is that Scouts are usually kind of sucked into this world

196
00:12:29,339 --> 00:12:33,060
where we're encouraged to spend an awful lot of energy

197
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:35,900
on the things that just don't matter.

198
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:39,060
Remember that the only things necessary for the game to happen

199
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:41,939
is the organizational concept of the game,

200
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:45,340
the rules of the game and players participating in it.

201
00:12:45,420 --> 00:12:46,820
If that's all that matters,

202
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:48,980
that's where we should be applying most of our energy.

203
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:53,140
If you go and you look on the sidelines of the of an organized game,

204
00:12:53,340 --> 00:12:58,180
you'll see a table. at that table is the game clock and the scorekeepers

205
00:12:58,180 --> 00:12:59,940
and the officials and everything.

206
00:12:59,940 --> 00:13:01,620
They're not necessary to the game.

207
00:13:01,620 --> 00:13:06,340
They provide a higher organizational concept to the simplicity of the game,

208
00:13:06,580 --> 00:13:09,340
but they're not absolutely necessary to the game.

209
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:11,700
Take that analogy and apply it to Scouting.

210
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:15,780
Things like advancement and the earning of maripages and ranks-

211
00:13:15,780 --> 00:13:17,460
They're all nice to have,

212
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:20,900
but they're not absolutely necessary to the playing of the game.

213
00:13:20,980 --> 00:13:22,980
It's just a way of keeping score.

214
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:25,460
We just use things like the advancement program

215
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:28,460
as a way to lend some structure and meaning to the game.

216
00:13:28,660 --> 00:13:29,660
But it's OK,

217
00:13:29,660 --> 00:13:31,380
You don't have to have it.

218
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:32,820
You could play the game without it.

219
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:38,060
You can develop character without ever completing a rank requirement,

220
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:40,620
Although the completion of rank requirements

221
00:13:40,660 --> 00:13:42,420
is a handy way of keeping score.

222
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:43,660
And while I'm on the soapbox,

223
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:47,660
I could point out any number of other things that serve as distractions

224
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:53,020
from the goal that we apply a lot of energy to. that just don't matter.

225
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:57,660
What matters is the scout that you're working with right now

226
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:02,660
and how they are progressing towards the goal of character development.

227
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:05,540
That's all that matters.

228
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:10,180
OK, soapbox session is done for today.

229
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:15,580
So what's important is we need to understand the place of the players

230
00:14:15,580 --> 00:14:18,660
in this game, that they are volunteering to play the game.

231
00:14:18,940 --> 00:14:23,340
They have a very simple motive that they may or may not be able to explain, real

232
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:25,300
while, but it corresponds with ours.

233
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:28,060
They're going to bring the energy of why they want to be in scouts

234
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:30,900
And we're going to take that energy and we're going to use it

235
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:34,700
in a transformational manner to help them develop character.

236
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:37,500
Up to this point, we've talked about perspective and motivation,

237
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,980
So now let's talk about the players themselves.

238
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:44,940
Our scouts will have different developmental needs.

239
00:14:45,420 --> 00:14:48,140
Our scouts will have different sets of skills.

240
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:50,660
Our scouts will be individuals,

241
00:14:50,820 --> 00:14:54,100
And part of the fascination of playing this game with them

242
00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:58,460
is beginning to appreciate two things: how we are all the same

243
